            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case: Monday Morning, Account Enters 'Restricted Review'</h2>
<p>Monday morning, 9 AM. Xiao Zhang, the performance marketer for a utility app overseas team, logged into the Meta ad dashboard as usual, ready to scale up. But all ad statuses showed 'Paused', and the account was under 'Restricted Review'. </p>
<p>Worse, there were still over $30,000 in the account balance, and the ads had been paused for nearly 48 hours.</p>
<p>Xiao Zhang checked recent notification emails and found a creative disapproval alert from three days earlier—but at the time, they thought it was just a single disapproved ad and didn't take it seriously. It turned out the system had automatically triggered an account-level review, pausing all ads.</p>
<hr />
<h2>The Problem: Three Issues Simultaneously</h2>
<p>Looking back, the root cause wasn't a single creative issue but a combination of three problems:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Creative Level</strong>: Several ad creatives used unauthorized phone screenshot interfaces, flagged as 'Misleading Content'</li>
<li><strong>Landing Page Level</strong>: The product page had several 'user reviews' suspected of being fake, violating platform 'Social Proof' policies</li>
<li><strong>Copy Level</strong>: Ad copy contained '100% free' claims, but the app actually had paid subscriptions—'Exaggerated Functionality'</li>
</ol>
<p>All three triggered the platform's compliance red lines simultaneously, leading to a systematic account review.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Diagnosis: This Wasn't 'Bad Luck'—It Was Systemic Risk</h2>
<p>Many teams would attribute this to 'bad luck' or 'stricter platform enforcement', but in reality, it reflects a <strong>systematic gap in compliance management</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>No pre-approval mechanism for creatives; single disapprovals were random, but batch disapprovals were inevitable</li>
<li>No habit of tracking platform policy changes; violation standards had updated but the team didn't know</li>
<li>No account alert mechanism; review notifications got buried in emails</li>
<li>Landing page and ad creatives were handled by different people with no unified compliance standards</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Actions: 48-Hour Emergency Response SOP</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Confirm Violation Reasons (0-2 Hours)</h3>
<p>Log into the ad dashboard, find 'Account Status' and 'Policy Center', and download all specific reasons for disapproved items. Create a problem list:</p>
<ul>
<li>Violation Type: Misleading Content × 5 items</li>
<li>Violation Type: Fake Reviews × 2 items</li>
<li>Violation Type: Exaggerated Functionality × 3 items</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 2: Creative Remediation (2-8 Hours)</h3>
<p>Handle each violation category by division:</p>
<ul>
<li>Design Team: Replace all unauthorized screenshots with custom UI mockups</li>
<li>Copy Team: Revise exaggerated claims to objective feature descriptions</li>
<li>Product Team: Remove all suspicious reviews from landing pages, keep only authentic ones</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 3: Submit Appeal (8-12 Hours)</h3>
<p>Submit an account appeal through Meta Business Help Center. The appeal should include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Explanation of violation issues</li>
<li>Completed remediation measures (with screenshots)</li>
<li>Compliance commitment letter (outlining future prevention plans)</li>
</ul>
<h3>Step 4: Audit Other Accounts (12-24 Hours)</h3>
<p>This team had 3 ad accounts; only 1 had issues this time. Did the other two accounts have the same risk points in their creatives and landing pages?</p>
<p>The team ran a quick scan using the same checklist and found 2 similar creatives in Account B—those were immediately paused.</p>
<h3>Step 5: Resume and Monitor (24-48 Hours)</h3>
<p>After the appeal was approved, the account was restored. Instead of resuming all ads immediately, the team first tested only confirmed-compliant creatives, controlled budgets, and observed for 48 hours before gradually scaling.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Outcome: Account Restored, But the Lesson Was Systematic</h2>
<p>After 48 hours, the account was back to normal. The $30,000+ balance was preserved, and ads resumed.</p>
<p>But the real change this case brought wasn't 'saved one account'—it was <strong>pushing the team to establish a compliance audit SOP</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Added 'Compliance Self-Check' step before creative launch</li>
<li>Fixed weekly account health audit every Friday</li>
<li>Designated someone to track platform policy updates</li>
<li>Unified compliance standards for landing pages and ad creatives</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>A Reminder: Compliance Audits Aren't 'Just a Checklist'</h2>
<p>Many teams know compliance matters but often treat it as 'check occasionally when we have time'.</p>
<p>In reality, platform policies are constantly evolving, creatives are continuously being produced, and violation risks are dynamic. One oversight might only disapprove a single creative, but accumulated violations will trigger account-level reviews.</p>
<p>Instead of emergency recovery after account suspension, make audits part of daily operations.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Yiyu Media Compliance Support Services</h2>
<p>When managing overseas ad accounts for teams, Yiyu Media includes compliance auditing as part of the service: <strong>pre-launch creative compliance review</strong>, <strong>real-time account status monitoring</strong>, and <strong>rapid response to violation issues</strong>. Help teams scale their ad spend while maintaining compliance standards.</p>
